      "text": "Since the center of a circle lies on the $x$-axis, the midpoint of the sides of the triangle $A(0,4), B(1,3), C(x,0)$ is located on the $x$-axis. The coordinates of the midpoint of $\\overline{AB}$ are $\\left( \\frac{0+1}{2}, \\frac{4+3}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{7}{2} \\right)$. Since this point lies on the $x$-axis, the $y$-coordinate is $0$. \n\nThe midpoint of $\\overline{AC}$ is located at $\\left( \\frac{x+0}{2}, \\frac{0+4}{2} \\right)= \\left( \\frac{x}{2}, 2 \\right)$.\n$\\frac{x}{2}=0$, hence $x=0 * Previous reasoning$. By subtracting 2 from both sides, we conclude that the $y$-coordinate of the midpoint is $0$. Since this point's $y$-coordinate is on the x-axis, the value of the midpoint is at $0$ (since this is the intersection with the x-axis), so the ratio is $\\boxed{\\text{5}/2}$.",
